<!--
    kuna `signedness`: an integer local whose every comparison is signed is
    declared unsigned, and the cast machinery writes the signedness back at each
    use.

    Datatype::type_order ranks SUB_UINT_PLAIN (16) ahead of SUB_INT_PLAIN (17),
    so in get_local_type's "keep the most specific" fold one uint vote outranks
    every int vote on the same Varnode, and nothing downstream re-decides.  On
    -O2 x86-64 those uint votes are structural: a strength-reduced loop bound is
    masked (INT_AND, which votes TYPE_UINT on both operands) even when every
    comparison on it is signed.

    Bytes at 0x1000, the reduced shape (three parameters so the masked value is
    declared rather than inlined at each use):

        1000  89 f8                 MOV  EAX,EDI
        1002  01 f0                 ADD  EAX,ESI
        1004  25 f8 ff ff 7f        AND  EAX,0x7ffffff8   ; the TYPE_UINT vote
        1009  39 c2                 CMP  EDX,EAX
        100b  7d 06                 JGE  0x1013           ; signed
        100d  01 c2                 ADD  EDX,EAX
        100f  39 c2                 CMP  EDX,EAX
        1011  7c fa                 JL   0x100d           ; signed
        1013  89 d0                 MOV  EAX,EDX
        1015  c3                    RET

    Stage: P9 emission, sub-stage "naming-policy" (the declaration seam, next
    to kuna_declhightype).  P0 assertion: `option signedness
    upstream|auto|prefer-signed|prefer-unsigned`.

    Pass 1 sets `option signedness upstream` explicitly - the declaration type
    inference produces, which is what this option's other values are measured
    against - and reproduces the defect: `uint4 v1;` declared, and the two signed
    comparisons carry a `(int4)` cast (asserts #1 and #2).
    Pass 2 (`option signedness auto`, the shipped default) declares `int4 v1;`
    (every signedness-sensitive reader of v1 demands signed and none demands
    unsigned) and the cast, now a no-op against that declaration, is dropped
    (asserts #3 and #4).

    The second function `g` at 0x1100 is the width guard, reduced from
    findutils `find -O2` sub_bac0.  C's integer promotions convert anything
    narrower than `int` to `int` before any operator runs, and the declaration
    decides which extension that is, so `==` and `!=`, which this pass treats
    as signedness-independent at a fixed width, are NOT independent below the
    promotion width: `(short)-1 == -1` is true while `(unsigned short)0xffff ==
    -1` is a constant false.  No operation in `g` settles v2's signedness, so
    `prefer-unsigned` would re-declare it if the pass were willing to touch a
    narrow local (and `if ((a0 == v2) || (v2 == -1))` would then contain a
    constant false, which is what reviewing the compiled C found).  It is not
    willing: assert #5 pins `int2 v2;` in both passes, and assert #6 pins that
    the same pass did re-declare the int-wide `v3`, so #5 is the width rule
    declining rather than an inactive option.

        1100  66 b8 01 00           MOV   AX,0x1
        1104  31 c9                 XOR   ECX,ECX
        1106  48 c7 c2 00 61 03 00  MOV   RDX,0x36100
        110d  83 c1 01              ADD   ECX,0x1
        1110  48 8d 72 10           LEA   RSI,[RDX + 0x10]
        1114  66 39 c7              CMP   DI,AX
        1117  74 0f                 JZ    0x1128
        1119  66 83 f8 ff           CMP   AX,-0x1
        111d  74 09                 JZ    0x1128
        111f  66 8b 42 20           MOV   AX,word ptr [RDX + 0x20]
        1123  48 89 f2              MOV   RDX,RSI
        1126  eb e5                 JMP   0x110d
        1128  89 c8                 MOV   EAX,ECX
        112a  c3                    RET
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1000">
89f801f025f8ffff7f39c27d0601c239c27cfa89d0c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x1100">
66b8010031c948c7c20061030083c101488d72106639c7740f6683f8ff7409668b42204889f2ebe589c8c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1000" name="f"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x1100" name="g"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>option signedness upstream</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option signedness auto</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option signedness upstream</com>
  <com>lo fu g</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option signedness prefer-unsigned</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="signedness #1: the masked loop bound is declared unsigned under option signedness upstream, the declaration type inference produces (bug reproduces, once)" min="1" max="1">^  uint4 v1; // eax$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="signedness #2: and every signed comparison on it carries a cast back to int4 (bug reproduces, once)" min="1" max="1">a2 &lt; \(int4\)v1</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="signedness #3: the shipped default, option signedness auto, declares it at the signedness its operations demand (fix, once)" min="1" max="1">^  int4 v1; // eax$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="signedness #4: and the cast the declaration makes a no-op is dropped (fix, once)" min="1" max="1">a2 &lt; v1;</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="signedness #5: a local narrower than int keeps its declaration in BOTH passes, because C promotes it before every operator (once per pass)" min="2" max="2">^  int2 v2;$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="signedness #6: while the int-wide locals in that same function do flip, so #5 is the width rule declining and not an inactive option (fix, once)" min="1" max="1">^  uint4 v3; // ecx$</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
